# Modules, packages, extensions and libraries

Four words name what reaches a program through `import`, and each names one
thing. A **module** is what a program imports, a **package** is what is
distributed and installed, an **extension** is the native code that
implements a package's modules, and a **library** is the compiled file that
code is loaded from. The rules are
[[01-modules|HQL-0024, modules]] ("Three
identities") and
[[01-packages|HQL-0057, packages]].

| Term | What it is | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| module | the canonical HQL name, a contract of declarations in one `mod.hql`, bound to one provider | `io.net`, `ml.metrics` |
| package | the distribution unit: a module root holding one or more modules, with the extension that implements them | `std`, `search`, `ml` |
| extension | the implementation unit: a descriptor, `id`, `abi` and `provides`, and the implementations of the external declarations of the modules it provides | `std`, `ml` |
| crate | the Cargo form of an installable package's source, named `hql-ext-<package>` | `hql-ext-ml` |
| library | the shared object an external extension is built into and loaded from | `libhql_ext_ml.dylib` |
| module root | a directory whose `<path>/mod.hql` files are modules, optionally with a `prelude.hql` | `lib/std/`, `modules/` of a crate |

A program names modules only. `import ml` never says which package provides
`ml`; the environment settles that, and a module has exactly one provider:
the runtime, when every declaration has an HQL body or it declares types
alone, or exactly one extension.

## Builtin and installable packages

A package is **builtin** or **installable**, by how it reaches the binary.

- A **builtin** package lives under `lib/` of the hql repository. Its
  contracts are bundled with the binary and its extension is compiled into
  it, so its extension is **internal**. There are two: `std`, the standard
  package, and `search`.
- An **installable** package is a crate built into a library and installed
  under `~/.hql/lib/<package>/`, or named by a workspace's
  `[packages] paths`. Its extension is **external**: it reaches the process
  by loading that library.

An installable package is **official** when its crate lives under `crates/`
of the [hql-extensions](https://github.com/ewiger/hql-extensions)
repository, **known** when its crate lives elsewhere and the package
registry admits it by name, and a **path** package when it is installed
from a directory on the user's machine. Installation by name reaches official
and known packages only.

A package carries one extension, named as the package is. An installable
package owns the top-level module namespace of its name: `ml` provides `ml`
and `ml.metrics` and nothing outside `ml`, and its name is neither a builtin
package's nor a builtin module's. A builtin package is not held to its
name: `std` provides `core`, `string`, `io` and the rest, and `search`
provides `lexical` and `semantic`.

## Three registries

"Registry" is qualified wherever it is written, because three things carry
the word.

| Name | What it holds | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| module registry | the modules of the environment, each with its contract | in the process, built from module roots |
| extension registry | the extensions active in the environment, each with its descriptor | in the process, built by extension discovery ([[09-packages-and-extensions|HQL-0024 part 9]]) |
| package registry | every official and known package, pinned to a source | `hql-packages.yaml` at the root of hql-extensions |

## The standard library

"The standard library" is the prose name of the standard package, `std`, and
stays in use. Elsewhere, "library" alone means the compiled file of an
external extension, never a package: a prebuilt library is a compiled
`.dylib`, `.so` or `.dll` for one hql release, not a package of its own.

See [[hql/extensions]] for how a contract is bound to its extension,
[[hql/std-lib]] for the standard package, and
[the packages audit](../../status/packages.hmd) for every package this hql
knows today.
